ese frustrations and develop coping strategies. Sometimes an outside perspective can be very beneficial.\n\n4. Self-Care: It\u2019s easy to get consumed by the needs of others. Make sure you\u2019re taking time for yourself to decompress and recharge.\n\n5. Seek Professional Advice: If your son\u2019s memory issues are concerning or if your grandpa\u2019s behavior is unusual, it might be a good idea to consult with a professional (like a pediatrician or geriatric specialist) to see if there are underlying issues that need addressing.\n\nIt\u2019s natural to feel frustrated, and acknowledging those feelings is an important step in managing them. Reminding yourself that their behaviors might not be intentional can help reduce some of the stress. If you ever feel overwhelmed, reaching out for additional support from friends, family, or professionals can be very beneficial.\n\nWould you like to discuss any specific concerns or steps further?"}
