l to set clear expectations for behavior and communication. For your kid, age-appropriate boundaries can help, and for your grandma, discussing what you need from her can be helpful. However, approach these conversations with empathy and understanding.\n\n5. **Seek Support**:\n   - Sometimes talking to a therapist or counselor can help you process your feelings and develop strategies to manage them.\n   - Support groups or family therapy might also be beneficial if the dynamics are challenging.\n\n6. **Self-care**:\n   - Take time for yourself. Engaging in activities you enjoy or that relax you can help recharge your emotional batteries.\n   - Physical exercise or hobbies can be great outlets for frustration.\n\nRemember, it's normal to feel frustration when caring for others, especially when they are an important part of your life. It's a sign that you care deeply and that you're invested in the relationship. Working through these emotions can lead to stronger connections and improved communication.\n\nIf you ever feel overwhelmed, it might be helpful to reach out to a mental health professional who can provide support tailored to your situation. You're not alone in this, and there are ways to work through it."}
